# Lab 05 – SCADA Human Machine Interface (HMI)

## Overview

This lab focused on building a basic SCADA Human Machine Interface (HMI) using FUXA and connecting it to an OpenPLC tank-filling simulation.

The HMI was designed to represent a simple tank process with:

- Tank level indication
- Motor-actuated fill valve
- Process piping
- Tank level value display
- Valve open/closed state indication

The HMI was connected to OpenPLC using Modbus TCP and tested with the running tank-fill simulation.

## Objectives

The objectives of this lab were to:

1. Create a basic tank process visualization in FUXA.
2. Add a tank level indicator and valve representation.
3. Connect FUXA to the OpenPLC simulation.
4. Configure PLC tags for the tank level and valve state.
5. Bind HMI objects to the corresponding PLC tags.
6. Run and observe the HMI while the simulated process was operating.

## Tools and Environment

- FUXA – SCADA/HMI platform
- OpenPLC – PLC simulation
- Modbus TCP – Communication protocol
- Oracle VirtualBox – Virtualization environment
- Ubuntu – SCADA environment

## PLC Tags

The HMI used the following process tags:

| Tag | Address | Type | Function |
|---|---|---|---|
| Tank Valve Open | 1 | Bool | Indicates whether the tank fill valve is open |
| Tank Level | 300081 | Int16 | Represents the tank level |

## HMI Design

The HMI was designed using a simple and restrained industrial visualization style. The main components include the tank, level indicator, valve, and connecting pipe.

The tank level indicator was configured with a range from 0 to 10 ft. The valve state changes according to the value of the `Tank Valve Open` tag.

## Testing

The completed HMI was connected to the OpenPLC tank-fill simulation and tested in runtime mode.

During testing:

- The tank level value was displayed on the HMI.
- The tank level changed according to the simulated process.
- The valve indication reflected the PLC valve state.
- The HMI successfully displayed the running process.

